            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I mean no offense when I say this, but it seems that the reference implementation is wrong in some way(?), and thus I implore you to reopen this issue.\n\nThere is nothing \"wrong\" per se. Judging by implementations, there are indeed two variations:\n- Ranges for `a` and `b` in OKLab are different: for JavaScript implementations it is below 1 (listed as `[-0.4, 0.4]`) while 'mini.colors' multiplies the range by 100. [This is documented](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/9e603a31d3fbc7ee61c9b556cd84e97fa2dcff53/doc/mini-colors.txt#L201-L202). This in turn results into chroma in OKLch being different by a factor of 100.\n- Lightness in 'mini.colors' is corrected [according to this approach](https://bottosson.github.io/posts/colorpicker/#intermission---a-new-lightness-estimate-for-oklab). During initial implementation of 'mini.colors' I remember going back and forth on whether this should be included and practical tests for common use cases (working with Neovim color schemes in general and making dark/light color scheme become light/dark in particular) showed subjectively better results *with* this adjustment. So it was added.\n\nWithout these two differences, the output of `MiniColors.convert({ l = 20, c = 0.01368, h = 253.09 }, 'hex')` is indeed `'#12161c'`. So, basically, with the following patch:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/lua/mini/colors.lua b/lua/mini/colors.lua\nindex 5fa0884..28eb375 100644\n--- a/lua/mini/colors.lua\n+++ b/lua/mini/colors.lua\n@@ -2119,11 +2119,11 @@ H.rgb2oklab = function(rgb)\n   if math.abs(B) < 1e-4 then B = 0 end\n \n   -- Normalize to appropriate range\n-  return { l = H.correct_lightness(100 * L), a = 100 * A, b = 100 * B }\n+  return { l = 100 * L, a = A, b = B }\n end\n \n H.oklab2rgb = function(lab)\n-  local L, A, B = 0.01 * H.correct_lightness_inv(lab.l), 0.01 * lab.a, 0.01 * lab.b\n+  local L, A, B = 0.01 * lab.l, lab.a, lab.b\n \n   local l_ = L + 0.3963377774 * A + 0.2158037573 * B\n   local m_ = L - 0.1055613458 * A - 0.0638541728 * B\n```\n\nAdjusting `a` / `b` / `c` components to the different range is mostly straightforward on user side (divide/mutliply by 100). As changing this in 'mini.colors' is a big breaking change, it won't be added.\n\nI'll take a look whether adding an option like `adjust_oklab_l` to `convert()` is reasonable. That way users can set it to get the behavior similar to those third party implementations.",
